{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kn1UHPJVW7zfuCcMczoUpx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/building-images/understanding-image-layers/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-24T01:05:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Docker Inc. — official product documentation\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D1.4\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"image-layers\", \"immutability\", \"base-image\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Understanding image layers — Docker documentation\n9\t\n10\t## Composition and immutability\n11\t\n12\t\"container images are composed of layers. And each of these layers, once created, are\n13\timmutable.\"\n14\t\n15\t\"Each layer in an image contains a set of filesystem changes - additions, deletions,\n16\tor modifications.\"\n17\t\n18\t## Reuse and dedup — SOUNDINGS Q4 ANSWER KEY\n19\t\n20\t\"This is beneficial because it allows layers to be reused between images.\"\n21\t\n22\tReuse makes \"builds faster and reduce the amount of storage and bandwidth required to\n23\tdistribute the images.\"\n24\t\n25\t## Union filesystem — DEPTH\n26\t\n27\t\"When you run a container from an image, a union filesystem is created where layers are\n28\tstacked on top of each other, creating a new and unified view.\"\n29\t\n30\t\"When the union filesystem is created, in addition to the image layers, a directory is\n31\tcreated specifically for the running container. This allows the container to make\n32\tfilesystem changes while allowing the original image layers to remain untouched.\"\n33\t\n34\tThe second sentence is a strong, sourced support for §2's immutability argument: the\n35\timage layers are never mutated even by a running container.\n36\t\n37\t## Layer ordering\n38\t\n39\t[EXTRACTOR SUMMARY — NOT VERBATIM] The page demonstrates ordering with a five-layer\n40\texample, showing later layers (application code) depending on foundational layers\n41\t(the runtime). Re-verify before quoting.\n42\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"714f732e-6165-42bf-8392-8c603f3d60fe","uuid":"97959740-1f69-4a7d-ab7d-036d3ce04679","timestamp":"2026-08-24T19:11:49.044Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\docker-docs-image-layers-2026-08-24.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/building-images/understanding-image-layers/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-24T01:05:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Docker Inc. — official product documentation\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D1.4\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"image-layers\", \"immutability\", \"base-image\"]\n---\n# Understanding image layers — Docker documentation\n\n## Composition and immutability\n\n\"container images are composed of layers. And each of these layers, once created, are\nimmutable.\"\n\n\"Each layer in an image contains a set of filesystem changes - additions, deletions,\nor modifications.\"\n\n## Reuse and dedup — SOUNDINGS Q4 ANSWER KEY\n\n\"This is beneficial because it allows layers to be reused between images.\"\n\nReuse makes \"builds faster and reduce the amount of storage and bandwidth required to\ndistribute the images.\"\n\n## Union filesystem — DEPTH\n\n\"When you run a container from an image, a union filesystem is created where layers are\nstacked on top of each other, creating a new and unified view.\"\n\n\"When the union filesystem is created, in addition to the image layers, a directory is\ncreated specifically for the running container. This allows the container to make\nfilesystem changes while allowing the original image layers to remain untouched.\"\n\nThe second sentence is a strong, sourced support for §2's immutability argument: the\nimage layers are never mutated even by a running container.\n\n## Layer ordering\n\n[EXTRACTOR SUMMARY — NOT VERBATIM] The page demonstrates ordering with a five-layer\nexample, showing later layers (application code) depending on foundational layers\n(the runtime). Re-verify before quoting.\n","numLines":42,"startLine":1,"totalLines":42}}}
