{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014asHq5nuXJs7GZEBB4wkWM","type":"tool_result","content":"862\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/objective-coverage.md ===\n863\t# KCNA — Objective Coverage Log\n864\t\n865\tTracks which exam competency each chapter covers, at what depth, and when it was last\n866\treviewed. Seeded at Chapter 1 with the full competency registry so it is useful from\n867\tChapter 2 onward.\n868\t\n869\t**Identifier scheme:** `D<domain>.<competency>` — the Lodestar convention established in\n870\tB1. CNCF publishes four domains and thirteen named competencies with **no numbering and\n871\tno sub-weights**. Every per-chapter weight figure is authored judgment and must be\n872\tdisclosed as such in front matter.\n873\t\n874\t---\n875\t\n876\t## ⚑ Competency count: 13, not 12\n877\t\n878\t`chapter-lineup.md` (B2, ratified) states \"12 competencies\" and \"twelve named\n879\tcompetencies,\" twice. **This is wrong.** The cached CNCF curriculum enumerates\n880\t4 + 4 + 2 + 3 = **13**, and B1's own `D1.1`–`D4.3` scheme yields 13 identifiers.\n881\tChapter 1's domain table is correct.\n882\t\n883\t**Correct B2 before Chapter 19's synthesis, the blueprint appendix, or the front-matter\n884\tdisclosure inherits the wrong figure.** (Author action — outside Stage 14's write scope.)\n885\t\n886\tSources: `lf-kcna-exam-page-2026-08-23`, `cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23`.\n887\t\n888\t---\n889\t\n890\t## Coverage by chapter\n891\t\n892\t| Objective | First covered in | Depth | Last reviewed |\n893\t|---|---|---|---|\n894\t| — (none) | Chapter 1 | n/a — orientation chapter, 0% weight, `objectives: []` | 2026-08-24 |\n895\t\n896\tChapter 1 covers no exam objectives. It establishes exam mechanics, the 2025 blueprint\n897\trestructure, and the cloud-native framing that Chapter 17 later harvests. It does\n898\t**name** roughly twenty terms belonging to later competencies without teaching them —\n899\tthose are tracked as reserved entries in `glossary.md`, not as coverage.\n900\t\n901\t---\n902\t\n903\t## Competency registry (13) — planned homes from B2\n904\t\n905\t| ID | Competency | Domain (weight) | Planned chapter(s) | Status |\n906\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n907\t| D1.1 | Kubernetes Core Concepts | Kubernetes Fundamentals (44%) | Ch 3, 4, 5, 6 | not yet covered |\n908\t| D1.2 | Administration | Kubernetes Fundamentals (44%) | Ch 8 | not yet covered |\n909\t| D1.3 | Scheduling | Kubernetes Fundamentals (44%) | Ch 7 | not yet covered |\n910\t| D1.4 | Containerization | Kubernetes Fundamentals (44%) | Ch 2 | not yet covered |\n911\t| D2.1 | Networking | Container Orchestration (28%) | Ch 9, 10 | not yet covered |\n912\t| D2.2 | Security | Container Orchestration (28%) | Ch 12 (+ Ch 10 boundary) | not yet covered |\n913\t| D2.3 | Troubleshooting | Container Orchestration (28%) | Ch 13 | not yet covered |\n914\t| D2.4 | Storage | Container Orchestration (28%) | Ch 11 | not yet covered |\n915\t| D3.1 | Application Delivery | Cloud Native Application Delivery (16%) | Ch 14, 15 | not yet covered |\n916\t| D3.2 | Debugging | Cloud Native Application Delivery (16%) | Ch 16 | not yet covered |\n917\t| D4.1 | Observability | Cloud Native Architecture (12%) | Ch 18 | not yet covered |\n918\t| D4.2 | Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles | Cloud Native Architecture (12%) | Ch 17 | not yet covered |\n919\t| D4.3 | Cloud Native Community and Collaboration | Cloud Native Architecture (12%) | Ch 17 | not yet covered |\n920\t\n921\t**Note on D4.3:** B1 flags it as the competency technically-strong candidates most\n922\treliably under-study. B2's mitigation is not a separate chapter but explicit treatment\n923\tinside Ch 17 — its own numbered sections, Fixed Points, and Soundings coverage — plus\n924\tdisproportionate representation in the Ch 19 synthesis and the Ch 20 mock. Track that\n925\tthis actually happens.\n926\t\n927\t**Curriculum typo worth recording:** the CNCF-published `KCNA_Curriculum.pdf` contains\n928\t\"Could Native Community and Collaboration\" for D4.3. Candidates who download it will see\n929\tit. Belongs in the blueprint appendix.\n930\t=== END WRITE ===\n931\t\n932\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/retrieval-log.md ===\n933\t# KCNA — Retrieval-Practice Ledger\n934\t\n935\tTracks which earlier-chapter topics have been retrieval-tested, and in which later\n936\tchapter. Also records forward commitments the prose has made to the reader, which are\n937\tbinding contracts rather than preferences.\n938\t\n939\t---\n940\t\n941\t## Retrieval items by chapter\n942\t\n943\t| Tested topic | Original chapter | Retested in |\n944\t|---|---|---|\n945\t| *(none — Chapter 1 is excluded from the retrieval schedule by design)* | — | — |\n946\t\n947\t**Chapter 1: 0 retrieval items, which is correct.** B3 excludes Ch 1 entirely — the\n948\tskill's table assumes Ch 1 is a content chapter, but here it is orientation at 0%\n949\tweight. The ramp begins at **Ch 3 (10%, drawing from Ch 2)**. The question-quality audit\n950\tconfirmed 0%/0% compliance.\n951\t\n952\t---\n953\t\n954\t## ⚑ Forward commitments — binding\n955\t\n956\t| # | Commitment | Where stated | Status |\n957\t|---|---|---|---|\n958\t| 1 | **Chapter 13's checkpoint must carry a Chapter 8 retrieval item** (version skew, framed as a troubleshooting cause) | Ch 1 §5, in plain reader-facing prose; QC2.2 makes the pairing a whole question | **OPEN — verify at Ch 13 Stage 13** |\n959\t\n960\t**Why #1 is a contract, not a preference.** Chapter 1 tells the reader the pairing by\n961\tname, then builds a checkpoint question around it whose distractor C asserts (correctly,\n962\tper B2) that Ch 13 does not *depend* on Ch 8. B3 schedules it deliberately: version skew\n963\tis \"the densest pure-recall material in the book, taught at the 40% mark and otherwise\n964\tnever revisited before exam day,\" and Ch 13 ← Ch 8 is exactly 5 chapters back, clearing\n965\tB3's spacing floor.\n966\t\n967\tIf Ch 13 ships without it, Chapter 1 has told the reader something false about the book,\n968\tin the section whose entire purpose is teaching them to trust the mechanism. **Flag this\n969\tin Chapter 13's chapter-state so it isn't lost fifteen chapters from now.**\n970\t\n971\t---\n972\t\n973\t## Open item — a ★ Fixed Point that is never retrieved\n974\t\n975\tChapter 1 designates `44 / 28 / 16 / 12` as its single ★ Fixed Point and tells the\n976\treader to memorize it above everything else. B3 then excludes Ch 1 from retrieval\n977\tentirely and lists \"Ch 1 mechanics\" among four things that must *not* be retrieved\n978\tanywhere in the book.\n979\t\n980\tNet effect: **the book's most emphatically flagged must-memorize fact is the one fact\n981\tnever retrieval-tested.** Defensible — the weights are reinforced structurally (Parts\n982\tII–V are named for the domains, `the-lodestar.md` carries them, Ch 19 synthesizes\n983\tagainst them, Ch 20 is weighted to them) — but it sits oddly beside §5, where Chapter 1\n984\tspends four paragraphs selling retrieval as \"the single highest-leverage thing this book\n985\tdoes structurally.\"\n986\t\n987\t**Referred for author decision.** Cheapest resolution: let the weights be retrieved\n988\t*instrumentally* rather than as trivia — a Ch 19 item requiring the reader to allocate\n989\tremaining study time across domains uses the weights without testing them as facts, and\n990\tstays inside B3's exclusion.\n991\t\n992\t---\n993\t\n994\t## ⚑ PROVISIONAL — B3 schedule summary (NOT from the B3 artifact)\n995\t\n996\t> **Provenance warning.** `book-outline/retrieval-architecture.md` on disk is a\n997\t> **stage-failure notice**, not the B3 document — the artifact was composed but a\n998\t> permission error prevented the write. What follows is transcribed from the prose\n999\t> summary embedded in that notice. It is detailed enough to have verified the Ch 13 ←\n1000\t> Ch 8 contract above, but it is **second-hand and must not be treated as canon.**\n1001\t>\n1002\t> **Re-run B3 with write access to `.pipeline-state/` before Chapter 3**, which is where\n1003\t> the schedule actually starts. Replace this block with the real artifact's figures.\n1004\t\n1005\t**Spacing targets.** Ch 3 at 10%, Ch 4 at 15%, then 20–25% through Ch 18. Five chapters\n1006\tsit at the 25% ceiling — Ch 13 and 16 (troubleshooting/debugging arc), Ch 15 and 17 (the\n1007\ttwo Zeniths), Ch 18 (last content chapter, most accumulated decay).\n1008\t\n1009\t**Three structural decisions:**\n1010\t1. Ch 1 excluded from retrieval entirely (orientation, 0% weight). Ch 3's 10% draws from\n1011\t   Ch 2; no item anywhere tests exam mechanics.\n1012\t2. Soundings are excluded from the budget but do the work anyway — Part 11 requires they\n1013\t   be answerable from prerequisites, which in this book means earlier chapters. Counting\n1014\t   them would distort their calibration purpose. Drafting instruction: source Soundings\n1015\t   from B2's Prerequisites column, making the spacing free.\n1016\t3. **Spacing floor:** from Ch 8 on, at least one item must come from ≥4 chapters back.\n1017\t   Without it the schedule degenerates into \"test the previous chapter.\"\n1018\t\n1019\t**Nine cross-cutting themes**, headed by the control loop (Ch 3 → 4 → 6 → 11 → 15 → 17)\n1020\tand namespaced-vs-cluster-scoped (which *derives* the RBAC four-way matrix instead of\n1021\tmemorizing it). One is a pattern rather than a fact: **\"the object exists but nothing\n1022\thappens without the component\"** — Ingress without a controller, NetworkPolicy on an\n1023\tunsupporting CNI, `kubectl top` without metrics-server, VPA not shipped by default.\n1024\tNaming it once and retrieving it by name turns four gotchas into one rule.\n1025\t\n1026\t**Decay fixes:** Ch 8's version skew → retrieved in Ch 13 (as a troubleshooting cause);\n1027\trelease cadence → Ch 17 (where the three-supported-minors rule and the ~3/year cadence\n1028\texplain each other). Ch 7 (scheduling) and Ch 14 (Helm) are similarly thin and get named\n1029\tanchors.\n1030\t\n1031\t**Four things that must NOT be retrieved:**\n1032\t1. Ch 1 exam mechanics\n1033\t2. The dated graduated-project roster (retrieve the maturity *levels* instead)\n1034\t3. The unpublished 60-question / 75% figures\n1035\t4. Any `[inferred]` trap framed as exam frequency\n1036\t\n1037\t---\n1038\t\n1039\t## Unresolved convention\n1040\t\n1041\t**The `[retrieval: chN]` tag's rendered form** — reader-visible or draft-only annotation\n1042\t— is still undecided. Chapter 1 correctly carries none, but §5 describes the mechanism\n1043\tto readers in prose, so the decision has a reader-facing dimension. **Chapter 3 is the\n1044\tfirst chapter that needs it settled.**\n1045\t=== END WRITE ===\n1046\t```"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"714f732e-6165-42bf-8392-8c603f3d60fe","uuid":"5723711c-ca4b-4f83-9da5-40772b7d0aa6","timestamp":"2026-08-24T19:12:15.780Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-01\\kb-manifest.md","content":"=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/objective-coverage.md ===\n# KCNA — Objective Coverage Log\n\nTracks which exam competency each chapter covers, at what depth, and when it was last\nreviewed. Seeded at Chapter 1 with the full competency registry so it is useful from\nChapter 2 onward.\n\n**Identifier scheme:** `D<domain>.<competency>` — the Lodestar convention established in\nB1. CNCF publishes four domains and thirteen named competencies with **no numbering and\nno sub-weights**. Every per-chapter weight figure is authored judgment and must be\ndisclosed as such in front matter.\n\n---\n\n## ⚑ Competency count: 13, not 12\n\n`chapter-lineup.md` (B2, ratified) states \"12 competencies\" and \"twelve named\ncompetencies,\" twice. **This is wrong.** The cached CNCF curriculum enumerates\n4 + 4 + 2 + 3 = **13**, and B1's own `D1.1`–`D4.3` scheme yields 13 identifiers.\nChapter 1's domain table is correct.\n\n**Correct B2 before Chapter 19's synthesis, the blueprint appendix, or the front-matter\ndisclosure inherits the wrong figure.** (Author action — outside Stage 14's write scope.)\n\nSources: `lf-kcna-exam-page-2026-08-23`, `cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23`.\n\n---\n\n## Coverage by chapter\n\n| Objective | First covered in | Depth | Last reviewed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| — (none) | Chapter 1 | n/a — orientation chapter, 0% weight, `objectives: []` | 2026-08-24 |\n\nChapter 1 covers no exam objectives. It establishes exam mechanics, the 2025 blueprint\nrestructure, and the cloud-native framing that Chapter 17 later harvests. It does\n**name** roughly twenty terms belonging to later competencies without teaching them —\nthose are tracked as reserved entries in `glossary.md`, not as coverage.\n\n---\n\n## Competency registry (13) — planned homes from B2\n\n| ID | Competency | Domain (weight) | Planned chapter(s) | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| D1.1 | Kubernetes Core Concepts | Kubernetes Fundamentals (44%) | Ch 3, 4, 5, 6 | not yet covered |\n| D1.2 | Administration | Kubernetes Fundamentals (44%) | Ch 8 | not yet covered |\n| D1.3 | Scheduling | Kubernetes Fundamentals (44%) | Ch 7 | not yet covered |\n| D1.4 | Containerization | Kubernetes Fundamentals (44%) | Ch 2 | not yet covered |\n| D2.1 | Networking | Container Orchestration (28%) | Ch 9, 10 | not yet covered |\n| D2.2 | Security | Container Orchestration (28%) | Ch 12 (+ Ch 10 boundary) | not yet covered |\n| D2.3 | Troubleshooting | Container Orchestration (28%) | Ch 13 | not yet covered |\n| D2.4 | Storage | Container Orchestration (28%) | Ch 11 | not yet covered |\n| D3.1 | Application Delivery | Cloud Native Application Delivery (16%) | Ch 14, 15 | not yet covered |\n| D3.2 | Debugging | Cloud Native Application Delivery (16%) | Ch 16 | not yet covered |\n| D4.1 | Observability | Cloud Native Architecture (12%) | Ch 18 | not yet covered |\n| D4.2 | Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles | Cloud Native Architecture (12%) | Ch 17 | not yet covered |\n| D4.3 | Cloud Native Community and Collaboration | Cloud Native Architecture (12%) | Ch 17 | not yet covered |\n\n**Note on D4.3:** B1 flags it as the competency technically-strong candidates most\nreliably under-study. B2's mitigation is not a separate chapter but explicit treatment\ninside Ch 17 — its own numbered sections, Fixed Points, and Soundings coverage — plus\ndisproportionate representation in the Ch 19 synthesis and the Ch 20 mock. Track that\nthis actually happens.\n\n**Curriculum typo worth recording:** the CNCF-published `KCNA_Curriculum.pdf` contains\n\"Could Native Community and Collaboration\" for D4.3. Candidates who download it will see\nit. Belongs in the blueprint appendix.\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/retrieval-log.md ===\n# KCNA — Retrieval-Practice Ledger\n\nTracks which earlier-chapter topics have been retrieval-tested, and in which later\nchapter. Also records forward commitments the prose has made to the reader, which are\nbinding contracts rather than preferences.\n\n---\n\n## Retrieval items by chapter\n\n| Tested topic | Original chapter | Retested in |\n|---|---|---|\n| *(none — Chapter 1 is excluded from the retrieval schedule by design)* | — | — |\n\n**Chapter 1: 0 retrieval items, which is correct.** B3 excludes Ch 1 entirely — the\nskill's table assumes Ch 1 is a content chapter, but here it is orientation at 0%\nweight. The ramp begins at **Ch 3 (10%, drawing from Ch 2)**. The question-quality audit\nconfirmed 0%/0% compliance.\n\n---\n\n## ⚑ Forward commitments — binding\n\n| # | Commitment | Where stated | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Chapter 13's checkpoint must carry a Chapter 8 retrieval item** (version skew, framed as a troubleshooting cause) | Ch 1 §5, in plain reader-facing prose; QC2.2 makes the pairing a whole question | **OPEN — verify at Ch 13 Stage 13** |\n\n**Why #1 is a contract, not a preference.** Chapter 1 tells the reader the pairing by\nname, then builds a checkpoint question around it whose distractor C asserts (correctly,\nper B2) that Ch 13 does not *depend* on Ch 8. B3 schedules it deliberately: version skew\nis \"the densest pure-recall material in the book, taught at the 40% mark and otherwise\nnever revisited before exam day,\" and Ch 13 ← Ch 8 is exactly 5 chapters back, clearing\nB3's spacing floor.\n\nIf Ch 13 ships without it, Chapter 1 has told the reader something false about the book,\nin the section whose entire purpose is teaching them to trust the mechanism. **Flag this\nin Chapter 13's chapter-state so it isn't lost fifteen chapters from now.**\n\n---\n\n## Open item — a ★ Fixed Point that is never retrieved\n\nChapter 1 designates `44 / 28 / 16 / 12` as its single ★ Fixed Point and tells the\nreader to memorize it above everything else. B3 then excludes Ch 1 from retrieval\nentirely and lists \"Ch 1 mechanics\" among four things that must *not* be retrieved\nanywhere in the book.\n\nNet effect: **the book's most emphatically flagged must-memorize fact is the one fact\nnever retrieval-tested.** Defensible — the weights are reinforced structurally (Parts\nII–V are named for the domains, `the-lodestar.md` carries them, Ch 19 synthesizes\nagainst them, Ch 20 is weighted to them) — but it sits oddly beside §5, where Chapter 1\nspends four paragraphs selling retrieval as \"the single highest-leverage thing this book\ndoes structurally.\"\n\n**Referred for author decision.** Cheapest resolution: let the weights be retrieved\n*instrumentally* rather than as trivia — a Ch 19 item requiring the reader to allocate\nremaining study time across domains uses the weights without testing them as facts, and\nstays inside B3's exclusion.\n\n---\n\n## ⚑ PROVISIONAL — B3 schedule summary (NOT from the B3 artifact)\n\n> **Provenance warning.** `book-outline/retrieval-architecture.md` on disk is a\n> **stage-failure notice**, not the B3 document — the artifact was composed but a\n> permission error prevented the write. What follows is transcribed from the prose\n> summary embedded in that notice. It is detailed enough to have verified the Ch 13 ←\n> Ch 8 contract above, but it is **second-hand and must not be treated as canon.**\n>\n> **Re-run B3 with write access to `.pipeline-state/` before Chapter 3**, which is where\n> the schedule actually starts. Replace this block with the real artifact's figures.\n\n**Spacing targets.** Ch 3 at 10%, Ch 4 at 15%, then 20–25% through Ch 18. Five chapters\nsit at the 25% ceiling — Ch 13 and 16 (troubleshooting/debugging arc), Ch 15 and 17 (the\ntwo Zeniths), Ch 18 (last content chapter, most accumulated decay).\n\n**Three structural decisions:**\n1. Ch 1 excluded from retrieval entirely (orientation, 0% weight). Ch 3's 10% draws from\n   Ch 2; no item anywhere tests exam mechanics.\n2. Soundings are excluded from the budget but do the work anyway — Part 11 requires they\n   be answerable from prerequisites, which in this book means earlier chapters. Counting\n   them would distort their calibration purpose. Drafting instruction: source Soundings\n   from B2's Prerequisites column, making the spacing free.\n3. **Spacing floor:** from Ch 8 on, at least one item must come from ≥4 chapters back.\n   Without it the schedule degenerates into \"test the previous chapter.\"\n\n**Nine cross-cutting themes**, headed by the control loop (Ch 3 → 4 → 6 → 11 → 15 → 17)\nand namespaced-vs-cluster-scoped (which *derives* the RBAC four-way matrix instead of\nmemorizing it). One is a pattern rather than a fact: **\"the object exists but nothing\nhappens without the component\"** — Ingress without a controller, NetworkPolicy on an\nunsupporting CNI, `kubectl top` without metrics-server, VPA not shipped by default.\nNaming it once and retrieving it by name turns four gotchas into one rule.\n\n**Decay fixes:** Ch 8's version skew → retrieved in Ch 13 (as a troubleshooting cause);\nrelease cadence → Ch 17 (where the three-supported-minors rule and the ~3/year cadence\nexplain each other). Ch 7 (scheduling) and Ch 14 (Helm) are similarly thin and get named\nanchors.\n\n**Four things that must NOT be retrieved:**\n1. Ch 1 exam mechanics\n2. The dated graduated-project roster (retrieve the maturity *levels* instead)\n3. The unpublished 60-question / 75% figures\n4. Any `[inferred]` trap framed as exam frequency\n\n---\n\n## Unresolved convention\n\n**The `[retrieval: chN]` tag's rendered form** — reader-visible or draft-only annotation\n— is still undecided. Chapter 1 correctly carries none, but §5 describes the mechanism\nto readers in prose, so the decision has a reader-facing dimension. **Chapter 3 is the\nfirst chapter that needs it settled.**\n=== END WRITE ===\n```","numLines":185,"startLine":862,"totalLines":1058}}}
