{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SU8s5H8dBCn6pB2nfR7jNW","type":"tool_result","content":"933\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/namespace.md ===\n934\t# Concept: Namespaces — a scope for names\n935\t\n936\t**Home:** Chapter 4 §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n937\t\n938\t## Definition (verbatim)\n939\t\n940\t> In Kubernetes, **namespaces** provide a mechanism for isolating groups of resources\n941\t> within a single cluster. **Names of resources need to be unique within a namespace, but\n942\t> not across namespaces.** [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n943\t\n944\tChapter 4's compression: **a namespace is a scope for names.** \"Two ships on two different\n945\tregistries can carry the same name, and neither registry has to care.\"\n946\t\n947\t## Two structural constraints, both testable\n948\t\n949\t**Namespaces cannot be nested inside one another**, and **each Kubernetes resource can only\n950\tbe in one namespace.** [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n951\t\n952\t> 🪝 **Snag:** the instinct to build a hierarchy comes from cloud IAM (management groups →\n953\t> subscriptions → resource groups) and from Linux control-group trees. The platform will\n954\t> not accommodate it. **There is exactly one level.**\n955\t\n956\t## The restraint the documentation actually states\n957\t\n958\tPreparation material tends to present namespaces as a maturity signal. The documentation\n959\tdoes not: they are \"intended for use in environments with many users spread across\n960\tmultiple teams, or projects. For clusters with a few to tens of users, **you should not\n961\tneed to create or think about namespaces at all.** Start using them when you need the\n962\tfeatures they provide.\" For production, the guidance is to **not** use `default` — \"make"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"714f732e-6165-42bf-8392-8c603f3d60fe","uuid":"66e20157-7bbb-4971-8fe5-3f3e1eff231e","timestamp":"2026-08-24T19:13:49.063Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-04\\kb-manifest.md","content":"=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/namespace.md ===\n# Concept: Namespaces — a scope for names\n\n**Home:** Chapter 4 §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> In Kubernetes, **namespaces** provide a mechanism for isolating groups of resources\n> within a single cluster. **Names of resources need to be unique within a namespace, but\n> not across namespaces.** [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n\nChapter 4's compression: **a namespace is a scope for names.** \"Two ships on two different\nregistries can carry the same name, and neither registry has to care.\"\n\n## Two structural constraints, both testable\n\n**Namespaces cannot be nested inside one another**, and **each Kubernetes resource can only\nbe in one namespace.** [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]\n\n> 🪝 **Snag:** the instinct to build a hierarchy comes from cloud IAM (management groups →\n> subscriptions → resource groups) and from Linux control-group trees. The platform will\n> not accommodate it. **There is exactly one level.**\n\n## The restraint the documentation actually states\n\nPreparation material tends to present namespaces as a maturity signal. The documentation\ndoes not: they are \"intended for use in environments with many users spread across\nmultiple teams, or projects. For clusters with a few to tens of users, **you should not\nneed to create or think about namespaces at all.** Start using them when you need the\nfeatures they provide.\" For production, the guidance is to **not** use `default` — \"make","numLines":30,"startLine":933,"totalLines":1658}}}
